welcome to the QuickStart tutorial for  manage kubernetes today we're going to  deploy a containerized application first  we'll need to enable the kubernetes  engine API on our project in the  navigation menu click on API and  services and then click enable api's and  services here search for kubernetes  engine and select it from the results on  the kubernetes engine API page click  enable to enable it on the project next  let's activate the cloud shell in our  console where we'll run our commands  once we're here we can specify the  default zone that we want the G cloud  and cube cuttle commands to use type G  cloud config set compute zone followed  by a zone for this demonstration let's  use US East 1 with our default zone set  let's create a kubernetes cluster a  cluster consists of at least one cluster  master and multiple worker machines  called nodes these nodes are compute  engine VMs that run the kubernetes  processes an application is deployed to  a cluster and runs on its nodes enter  the command G cloud container clusters  create and a cluster name once our  cluster is created  we'll need authentication credentials to  interact with it we'll get that with G  cloud container clusters get credentials  followed by the cluster name the get  credentials sub command will configure  queue cuddle the command-line interface  for running commands against kubernetes  clusters to use the cluster we just  created in any subsequent commands now  it's time to deploy a containerized  application to our cluster type cube  cuddle create deployment and a name for  our deployment let's go with hello  server  we provided a sample web application  called hello app we can specify its  google container registry URL with the -  - image tag our application is deployed  but if we want to see what it looks like  we'll need to expose it to the Internet  by creating a kubernetes service type  cube cuddle expose deployment followed  by our deployment name  hello server we'll specify a type load  bouncer a port 80 and a target port 8080  this means that the load balancer will  initialize the public port 80 to the  Internet and route traffic to port 8080  of the application after a few minutes  the load balancer should be all set up  and have an assigned external IP address  cube cuddle get serviced followed by our  deployment name will show us the IP  address we'll use to access our  application enter that IP address in a  new tab or window and there you have it  leads just deployed a containerized web  application to kubernetes  you  [Music]
 

